Leetcode - 198

🏠 Problem Statement

You have houses in a row, and each house has some amount of money.

You want to rob houses to get the maximum money, but:

You cannot rob two adjacent houses because the alarm will trigger.

Example:

arr = [2, 7, 9, 3, 1]

Best choice:

2 + 9 + 1 = 12

So answer = 12.


🧠 Intuition

When I'm standing at house i, I have two choices:

1. Don't rob house i

Then I keep the maximum money I had until the previous house:

arr[i-1]
2. Rob house i

If I rob house i, I cannot rob house i-1].

So I take:

arr[i-2] + arr[i]

Therefore:

maximum = max(
    don't rob current → arr[i-1],
    rob current → arr[i-2] + arr[i]
)


Logic :

This is a dynamic programming problem where I need to maximize the money robbed without robbing two adjacent houses.

For every house, I have two choices: either skip the current house and keep the maximum amount from the previous house, or rob the current house. If I rob it, I cannot rob the previous house, so I add the current amount to the maximum amount from two houses before.

I take the maximum of these two choices. In my implementation, I update the input array itself instead of using a separate DP array, so I am doing the DP in-place.


Code :

class Solution {
    public int rob(int[] arr) {
        if(arr.length==1){
            return arr[0];
        }
        arr[1]=Math.max(arr[0],arr[1]);
        for (int i = 2; i < arr.length; i++) {
            arr[i]=Math.max((arr[i]+arr[i-2]),arr[i-1]);
        }
        return arr[arr.length-1];
    }
}
